<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Memory of the World Programme</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1992">1992</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Memory of the World Register</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Memory of the World Programme is an initiative launched by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1992 to protect the world's documentary heritage and make it accessible. Its international register lists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, archives, books, films and recordings judged to be of world significance, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-the-joseon-dynasty">Annals of the Joseon Dynasty</a> and the surviving volumes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheca-corviniana">Bibliotheca Corviniana</a>. Regional and national registers support the same aim.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/memory-of-the-world-programme.jpg" alt="Memory of the World Programme" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UNESCO · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unesco_Memory_of_the_World_Logo_(English).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Annals of the Joseon Dynasty and the Diaries of the Royal Secretariat are among the most detailed state records of any premodern country and are inscribed on UNESCO's <b>Memory of the World</b> register.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Visegrád was one of the first centres of the Italian Renaissance outside Italy: Italian humanists, architects and artists worked there, and the royal library, the Bibliotheca Corviniana, held perhaps two thousand volumes, of which over two hundred survive and are listed in UNESCO's <b>Memory of the World</b> register.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Demotte Shahnameh was produced under the Ilkhanid sultan Abu Sa'id; three of Timur's grandsons, Baysunghur, Ibrahim Sultan and Mohammad Juki, each commissioned a copy, and the Baysonghori Shahnameh of 1430 is preserved intact at Golestan Palace in Iran, inscribed in UNESCO's <b>Memory of the World Register</b> in 2007.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
